WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Good morning, Hank; it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:03.870
It's question Tuesday, the day that I answer
real questions from real Nerdfighters; let's

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:04.870
get right to it.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.500
Why is Hamlet's father also named Hamlet?

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.950
Just some old-fashioned Shakespearean doubling,
by the way that'll be discussed in detail

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:11.440
in next weeks CrashCourse Literature.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.210
Do you think the TFiOS movie is actually good
or do you feel obliged to say that because

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.210
it's based off of your book?

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:20.470
That's a good question; to be totally honest
if I didn't like it I would still try to find

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:25.369
nice things to say about it, like "Oh, the
set design is amazing" or "Man, that Ansel

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:27.820
Elgort sure looks good in a Rik Smits jersey."

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.810
But, in fact, I actually love the movie.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:30.810
Now I might be wrong.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.290
I am not at all objective, but, yeah, I love
it.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:36.140
Do you think people who read are more empathetic
than those who don't?

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.140
Yeah, there are actually studies that show
a correlation between empathy and reading,

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:40.550
at least when it comes to literary fiction.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.650
Where do you stand on elbows?

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.200
I don't; I only stand on my feet.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.370
I'm not very coordinated.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.251
I don't watch your videos, why should I?

00:00:47.251 --> 00:00:50.750
Look, I don't want to criticize anyone but
that is a terrible question Tuesday question

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:56.379
because if I answer it, you definitionally
won't hear my justification for why you should

00:00:56.379 --> 00:00:59.149
watch my videos because you don't watch my
videos.

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:02.510
But this brings up a bigger question: How
do you get to the people that aren't inside

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:03.510
the camera?

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.999
Maybe there's no one inside the camera; maybe
I'm just staring into the void.

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:10.649
Oh god, I feel an existential crisis coming
on.

00:01:10.649 --> 00:01:11.689
Thoughts on the ocean?

00:01:11.689 --> 00:01:14.020
I don't know, the tide goes in, the tide goes
out.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.579
What does it even really matter anymore?

00:01:15.579 --> 00:01:19.039
Are there more stars in the universe or grains
of sand on earth?

00:01:19.039 --> 00:01:22.639
Well, see that's an interesting and beautiful
question that makes me grateful to be able

00:01:22.639 --> 00:01:24.170
to apprehend the universe.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.159
Suddenly I've been lifted out of my funk!

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:27.960
I think there are more stars.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.959
More stars than there are grains of sand on
earth and there are a lot of grains of sand!

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:34.429
Anyway you can find more info in the dooblydoo.

00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:36.560
How are you enjoying participating in The
Art Assignment?

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.209
Oh, it's so fun; it's the most fun I've had
in a long time.

00:01:39.209 --> 00:01:42.229
It actually reminds me of the early days of
Vlogbrothers because there's this tight-knit

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:46.719
community, like, doing projects together and
responding to each others work; it's just

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:47.819
really, really awesome.

00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:50.610
How excited are you for the Divergent movie
in two weeks?

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.679
I am "already-bought-my-tickets-for-opening-day"
excited.

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:56.049
What are your predictions for this years Indycar
season?

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:58.559
How did you guys find out that I'm a secret
fan of Indycar?

00:01:58.559 --> 00:02:02.240
Anyway I think the Indie 500 is gonna be won,
for the second time, by Tony Kanaan and I

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.229
think the series title is going to go to Will
Power, and yes that is his real name.

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:08.670
Can you say DFTBA in French?

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:09.670
I can try.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:13.049
(in a terrible stereotypical french accent)
D-F-T-B-A (laughs).

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:14.379
Why do writers use symbolism?

00:02:14.379 --> 00:02:17.010
OK, let's say that you have a headache and
you want to tell someone about it and you

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.150
say "I have a headache" and other people are
like "Yeah whatever, everybody gets headaches".

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.569
But your headache is not a regular headache;
it's a serious headache.

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:27.040
So you say "my brain is on fire" to try to
help these people understand that this is

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.459
a headache that needs attention.

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:29.629
That's a metaphor, right?

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:31.319
And you use it so that you can be understood.

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:34.159
Now let's say you want to take those same
imagistic principles but apply them to a much

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:38.280
more complex idea than having a headache,
like for instance, the yearning that one feels

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.500
for one's dreams.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.760
Then you can see the dream but you can't cross
the bay to get to the green light that embodies

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:45.760
your dream.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.489
And you want to talk about socio-economic
class in America is a barrier, a bay-like

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:53.780
barrier some would say, that stands between
you and the green light and makes that gap

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:54.780
unbridgeable.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.311
Now you can just talk about that stuff directly
but when you talk about it symbolically, it

00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:03.379
becomes more powerful because instead of being
abstract, it becomes kind of observable.

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:05.129
So I think that's why, all right, what's the
next question?

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:07.200
Who has the best butt- you or Hank?

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.439
I don't think either of us has the best butt;
the best butt I've ever

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:11.180
personally seen is actually Wil Wheaton's.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.620
Wait, did I just think that or did I say it
out loud?

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.400
Have you ever dreamed to be a pizza?

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.739
Mmm, no that's one of those dreams that seems
initially promising but in fact I think would

00:03:18.739 --> 00:03:19.739
be a horrible nightmare.

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:23.810
Like just imagine smelling like pizza and
knowing that you would taste like pizza but

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:28.310
then also knowing that if you eat yourself,
you will die and ergo not be able to ever

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:29.439
eat pizza again.

00:03:29.439 --> 00:03:31.890
What are you most excited about right now?

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:32.890
What a great question!

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:34.269
That's something I should ask myself everyday.

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:37.420
I think right now it's The Art Assignment,
but I wanna know what you're most excited

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.890
about, so let me know in comments.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.009
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

